# v0.5.0 — Iso-tower-parametric data, end-to-end

This release turns the v0.4.0 foundation arc into a **user-visible
capability**: Haskell-style data declarations like
`data List a = Nil | Cons a (List a)` now produce iso-tower-
parametric data, where each ctor application carries singleton-
precise typing.

## What's new

### End-to-end iso-tower-parametric

```
data Bool⋮ { T⋮; F⋮ };
data List a = Nil | Cons a (List a);
let lst = Cons T Nil
```

⇒ `lst :: Cons T Nil` — the covering-space framing made concrete.

Each value carries its own singleton type, with `List a` as the
implicit covering space.  Previously this would have been
`lst :: List T` (classical-parametric).

### Kind-level slidability + Haskell-style sugar in deferred-fn args

```
data Bool = T | F;
let pickT = \b -> case b { T -> T; F -> T };
data Wit : *0 { W : pickT T -> Wit };
let rt = W T
```

The Haskell-style sugar (`data Bool = T | F`) plus the iso-tower
desugar means `T : T`; R2's kind-level slidability gate accepts
the slide on `pickT T`; the value-level interpreter reduces it to
`T`; and `W T` typechecks against the reduced result.  All
without explicit `⋮` markers on the data declaration.

